1
00:01:30,150 --> 00:01:35,410
 Julius Caesar awaits the surrender of Vercingetorix, the Galic chief he has just defeated in battle.

2
00:01:35,410 --> 00:01:49,190
 Caesar's resounding victory wets his appetite for power.

3
00:01:49,190 --> 00:01:52,220
 From now on, that appetite will know no bounds.

4
00:02:39,060 --> 00:02:55,780
 Caesar dictates his commentary on the battle.

5
00:02:55,780 --> 00:02:57,780
 Just as I suspected, the Gauls were no match for my Roman legions.

6
00:02:57,780 --> 00:03:01,690
 I attacked them on all fronts, and they scattered like a flock of frightened chickens.

7
00:03:01,690 --> 00:03:04,090
 We captured the runaways at Elysia.

8
00:03:04,090 --> 00:03:07,200
 I wanted to proclaim that when I wage a campaign, there's no such thing as opposition.

9
00:03:08,390 --> 00:03:09,790
 All must bow to my allegiance.

10
00:03:11,550 --> 00:03:15,460
 The world knows fruits won't have the gall to prevent it.

11
00:03:15,600 --> 00:03:17,920
 If I have to, I'll crush every last one of them.

12
00:03:20,010 --> 00:03:21,090
 I want the world to know it.

13
00:03:48,250 --> 00:03:56,660
 You look pretty nice at your place, don't you think, huh?

14
00:03:56,720 --> 00:03:58,810
 Hey, you big boy, bring me that tree now.

15
00:04:02,820 --> 00:04:19,800
 About that is the question.

16
00:04:21,170 --> 00:04:23,350
 Public is really in danger this time,

17
00:04:23,390 --> 00:04:25,330
 but we, the distinguished members of the Senate,

18
00:04:25,430 --> 00:04:28,370
 will put a stop to that ambitious little General Donald Caesar.

19
00:04:33,630 --> 00:04:39,880
 No, give me that goose, it's mine.

20
00:04:39,960 --> 00:04:40,640
 I slept before you.

21
00:04:40,820 --> 00:04:41,100
 Always.

22
00:04:41,400 --> 00:04:57,090
 No, but Caesar's triumph in no time became the capital of the world.

23
00:04:57,090 --> 00:05:30,260
 Wheat is distributed to the people.

24
00:05:33,110 --> 00:05:37,720
 When you're lucky enough to be a Roman citizen, why work?

25
00:05:38,230 --> 00:05:43,270
 Especially when the city is sure to provide bread and games.

26
00:05:43,790 --> 00:05:46,870
 Caesar had two semicircular theaters built side by side.

27
00:06:01,290 --> 00:06:08,360
 At his signal, the two enormous buildings were swung around to form one huge circus.

28
00:06:11,540 --> 00:06:18,080
 His invention would open up a whole new world in the realm of public entertainment.

29
00:06:33,750 --> 00:06:35,490
 Morning, story, pay seven dollars.

30
00:07:00,100 --> 00:07:00,960
 You're first to make it.

31
00:07:06,170 --> 00:07:09,400
 Yeah, listen, what do you think of that?

32
00:07:09,400 --> 00:07:10,050
 Open up!

33
00:07:10,050 --> 00:08:23,000
 The buildings in the older section of the city were fire hazards.

34
00:08:23,000 --> 00:08:25,220
 Local firemen had their hands full.

35
00:08:25,220 --> 00:08:30,680
 The gift of eloquence, much like fire, needs to be fueled.

36
00:08:30,680 --> 00:08:35,190
 And like fire, it must depend on motion for existence and sheds light only by burning itself out.

37
00:08:35,190 --> 00:08:37,190
 Now, if it ever happens at fire...

38
00:08:37,190 --> 00:08:44,890
 Good day, good day. For the glory of Rome, we are going to build roads.

39
00:08:44,890 --> 00:08:49,710
 So simple, really. Look, some sand, a few pebbles, some flagstones.

40
00:08:49,710 --> 00:08:51,710
 They'll begin right here.

41
00:08:51,710 --> 00:08:56,090
 Now for a little demonstration. Watch me well. First a slab.

42
00:08:56,090 --> 00:09:02,100
 Well, no, that's not it. First the sand, then your slab. Some pebbles.

43
00:09:02,100 --> 00:09:05,140
 No, no, not the pebbles. The slab. I mean a pebble.

44
00:09:05,140 --> 00:09:09,940
 The roads will lead from this column. From this hub they'll run like spokes over the world.

45
00:09:09,940 --> 00:09:14,850
 Such glory for Rome. Of course for me.

46
00:09:14,850 --> 00:09:19,100
 But Great Caesar's going down!

47
00:09:19,100 --> 00:09:34,410
 The rules of the Roman road will proceed straight ahead, regardless of terrain.

48
00:09:34,410 --> 00:09:39,170
 Which, uh, could sometimes cause problems.

49
00:09:39,170 --> 00:10:39,850
 Construct a superb amphitheater.

50
00:10:39,850 --> 00:10:42,250
 But, Master, I've no plans for that, none at all.

51
00:10:42,250 --> 00:10:45,160
 We've no materials to use, all the flagstones are in the road.

52
00:10:45,160 --> 00:10:48,610
 Baa! Right away!

53
00:10:48,610 --> 00:11:12,290
 Go on, go on!

54
00:11:12,290 --> 00:11:21,230
 Now, listen here now, I wouldn't get inconvenienced or too much, tell me where's the road.

55
00:11:21,230 --> 00:11:24,990
 Uh, but, General, have you seen the magnificent amphitheater? We've built in its place.

56
00:11:24,990 --> 00:11:25,410
 Huh?

57
00:11:25,410 --> 00:11:29,250
 Do you agree?

58
00:11:29,250 --> 00:11:32,170
 Order of Caesar, you're to keep building!

59
00:11:32,170 --> 00:11:41,990
 Artists and soldiers will never agree.

60
00:11:41,990 --> 00:12:09,690
 So nice!

61
00:12:09,690 --> 00:12:13,090
 That feels good!

62
00:12:13,090 --> 00:12:20,030
 Nicer like that.

63
00:12:20,030 --> 00:12:26,060
 Wasn't it a good idea to build these hot baths, General?

64
00:12:26,320 --> 00:12:32,970
 It's all right, no need for discussion.

65
00:12:39,890 --> 00:12:45,430
 You ever tried again?

66
00:12:48,210 --> 00:12:50,660
 Just taste a bit of this water, it's excellent.

67
00:12:53,640 --> 00:12:57,370
 You've been ordered to Rome, Master Caesar's orders.

68
00:12:57,790 --> 00:12:59,300
 This chariot has been assigned to you.

69
00:12:59,400 --> 00:13:01,320
 Oh, no, no, none of these hot rods for me.

70
00:13:01,740 --> 00:13:02,430
 My litter.

71
00:13:03,360 --> 00:13:04,720
 These are a little puny.

72
00:13:05,820 --> 00:13:10,860
 I'll take, uh, that and that one.

73
00:13:10,860 --> 00:13:29,720
 Seating with my urban renewal plan will demolish the walls of the old city.

74
00:13:30,090 --> 00:13:31,630
 And rewrote the timer.

75
00:13:33,170 --> 00:13:34,850
 Rearrange the campus martius.

76
00:13:35,450 --> 00:13:38,540
 And construct the Roman Forum right here.

77
00:13:39,170 --> 00:13:42,000
 But Caesar, there isn't enough room.

78
00:13:42,670 --> 00:13:46,580
 What? Not enough room. It's up to us to make room.

79
00:13:51,400 --> 00:13:58,290
 Buildings are torn down, only to be put back together.

80
00:13:59,910 --> 00:14:01,190
 Lowers the unemployment rate.

81
00:14:01,190 --> 00:14:12,460
 Promises, promises, that's all you'll ever hear, my fellow Romans.

82
00:14:12,820 --> 00:14:15,720
 Unite against our oppressors. Strength in numbers.

83
00:14:16,060 --> 00:14:17,980
 It's ridiculous what's happening in our city.

84
00:14:18,440 --> 00:14:20,490
 It's enough of this tyranny.

85
00:14:21,050 --> 00:14:31,480
 With the help of my latest invention, you'll be freed from your tiring chores, you'll see.

86
00:14:31,970 --> 00:14:33,660
 Fine, all right, roll em!

87
00:14:34,280 --> 00:14:36,170
 Look here now, a brilliant stroke.

88
00:14:36,350 --> 00:14:39,010
 Ah, there now.

89
00:14:40,710 --> 00:14:41,650
 But what's going on?

90
00:14:41,730 --> 00:14:43,470
 Hold it, ah, wait, this isn't happening.

91
00:14:43,570 --> 00:14:44,730
 Oh, my stars and goddesses.

92
00:14:44,930 --> 00:14:48,090
 Jupiter's quackiness, oh, no, no, no, no, no, no, no!

93
00:14:48,330 --> 00:14:51,810
 In spite of one or two minor setbacks, work is proceeding normally.

94
00:14:52,250 --> 00:14:54,050
 Not much progress being made here.

95
00:14:54,280 --> 00:14:56,100
 All must be finished in eight months.

96
00:14:57,270 --> 00:14:58,950
 Caesar, uh, twelve months.

97
00:14:59,300 --> 00:15:00,200
 No, eight months.

98
00:15:00,200 --> 00:15:02,260
 Listen here now, Caesar.

99
00:15:02,560 --> 00:15:03,960
 And listen to Grandad.

100
00:15:04,330 --> 00:15:05,210
 Well, I'm listening.

101
00:15:06,130 --> 00:15:12,460
 Each year we have first spring, then summer, fall, then winter, followed by another spring, summer, and so forth and so on.

102
00:15:12,620 --> 00:15:17,360
 The full moon returns three times each season, in theory anyway, depending on the season.

103
00:15:17,540 --> 00:15:21,460
 That makes a total of 365 days, one year, 12 months, see?

104
00:15:21,830 --> 00:15:23,580
 I agree, that's the way it is.

105
00:15:23,760 --> 00:15:28,500
 And so, 46 years before the year one, our calendar, the Julian, was invented.

106
00:15:29,650 --> 00:15:31,970
 Oh, come and look.

107
00:15:33,140 --> 00:15:34,740
 After you.

108
00:15:34,740 --> 00:15:36,930
 My very latest invention.

109
00:15:37,710 --> 00:15:38,810
 A water clock.

110
00:15:40,740 --> 00:15:43,400
 A work of genius, don't you think?

111
00:15:43,740 --> 00:15:44,660
 Fire! Fire!

112
00:15:56,820 --> 00:15:58,510
 Rain!

113
00:16:04,880 --> 00:16:47,680
 Don't be rewarded.

114
00:16:48,490 --> 00:16:50,610
 These are slaves, you can't just do that.

115
00:16:50,630 --> 00:16:50,910
 That's enough.

116
00:16:53,050 --> 00:16:54,650
 That's my business.

117
00:16:57,660 --> 00:16:58,740
 Settle it with your superiors.

118
00:16:58,760 --> 00:16:59,700
 Don't forget my rays.

119
00:17:13,720 --> 00:17:26,620
 Lucius Sergius' new house is built atop one of the city's seven hills.

120
00:17:27,860 --> 00:17:32,000
 Because of their bravery during the fire, our friends have a pleasant time of it there.

121
00:17:36,400 --> 00:17:40,040
 Red house, thanks.

122
00:17:40,240 --> 00:17:41,280
 Not bad, and you?

123
00:17:42,140 --> 00:17:44,020
 Coming, Pierrette.

124
00:17:50,160 --> 00:18:00,840
 Usually shaven, well, practically, Lucius Sergius is ready to receive his clientele.

125
00:18:00,840 --> 00:18:07,340
 They tell him their problems, and he dispenses counsel, recommendations, or even better, sportula.

126
00:18:07,680 --> 00:18:15,050
 Can be a few silver coins, a basket laden with food, as long as there is food to give.

127
00:18:37,920 --> 00:18:45,610
 Lucius, in turn, pays a visit to his patron to reassure him of his undying loyalty.

128
00:18:47,060 --> 00:18:53,380
 And they understand each other perfectly.

129
00:18:56,330 --> 00:18:58,880
 And how is, my dear friend?

130
00:18:59,080 --> 00:19:00,020
 Fine, quite fine, my friend.

131
00:19:20,790 --> 00:19:30,100
 That's it?

132
00:19:30,320 --> 00:19:34,360
 Go!

133
00:19:34,360 --> 00:19:36,480
 Pay for this!

134
00:19:36,480 --> 00:19:39,230
 Ha ha ha ha!

135
00:19:39,230 --> 00:19:41,920
 Ha ha ha ha ha ha!

136
00:19:41,920 --> 00:19:43,600
 Watch out!

137
00:19:43,600 --> 00:19:46,750
 What?

138
00:19:46,750 --> 00:20:02,690
 Human citizens spent a great deal of their time engaged in political discussion, and sometimes, in the most unlikely places.

139
00:20:02,690 --> 00:20:17,150
 See your future in the stars!

140
00:20:17,150 --> 00:20:19,150
 And this healthy young man! Let me read your part!

141
00:20:19,150 --> 00:20:21,650
 Who wants to buy my fine young man fresh off the ship from Nubia?

142
00:20:21,650 --> 00:20:23,000
 Hmm?

143
00:20:23,000 --> 00:20:24,990
 And how about this fair young lady?

144
00:20:24,990 --> 00:20:27,800
 Eh, my good friends, come see!

145
00:20:27,800 --> 00:20:33,690
 None other than my very latest invention, my column-driving machine!

146
00:20:36,000 --> 00:20:40,480
 It's absolute perfection, just brilliant, eh what? Here comes he!

147
00:20:40,480 --> 00:20:59,280
 Stop him! Mess! Stop it! Shut it up! Oh lordy, out of bed!

148
00:20:59,280 --> 00:21:06,110
 Evening falls. Dark streets are no longer safe.

149
00:21:06,110 --> 00:21:12,810
 The citizens of Rome return home, bar their doors, and make sure all valuables are safely inside.

150
00:21:12,810 --> 00:21:34,850
 After that, the only sounds echoing through the streets are the rumble of convoys laden with food and supplies for the city.

151
00:21:34,850 --> 00:21:55,170
 We're late, Pyrrha.

152
00:21:55,170 --> 00:21:55,470
 Hello.

153
00:22:10,320 --> 00:22:17,060
 Hard now.

154
00:22:17,680 --> 00:22:18,910
 We bust one length.

155
00:22:25,190 --> 00:22:29,750
 There's a ball suspended in the sky, which forms a solid hollow sphere around it.

156
00:22:29,790 --> 00:22:34,710
 Inside the sphere involves the sun shining on all the poor devils who live upside down on the other side of the earth.

157
00:22:35,390 --> 00:22:40,710
 All along the Tiber, where Pyrrha's gone for a stroll, can be found riches from all over the world.

158
00:22:41,010 --> 00:22:47,700
 Egyptian wheat, Spanish oil, lead, copper, silver, ivory, fabric, spices, silks.

159
00:22:47,700 --> 00:23:14,150
 But Pyrrha's master is waiting for him at the baths.

160
00:23:16,140 --> 00:23:21,750
 Good day, Ave, my partner's here.

161
00:23:26,650 --> 00:23:32,360
 Oh, no, no, no, no, no, no, no.

162
00:23:48,040 --> 00:24:05,170
 Stop, a bit higher there.

163
00:24:05,910 --> 00:24:06,890
 But you, hello.

164
00:24:10,880 --> 00:24:14,990
 But, wait for me.

165
00:24:15,110 --> 00:24:19,410
 The constellation of the stars predicts great changes for Rome.

166
00:24:19,430 --> 00:24:20,690
 Whatever am I doing here?

167
00:24:20,770 --> 00:24:23,250
 Yes, there will be great changes.

168
00:24:26,140 --> 00:24:33,800
 Jesus, I appreciate your concern, my friend, but I wouldn't dare.

169
00:24:34,570 --> 00:24:34,870
 No.

170
00:24:51,770 --> 00:25:23,390
 Decades later, a child was conceived whose birth would mark the year one of our modern era.

171
00:25:23,390 --> 00:25:34,590
 That birth would have an unparalleled impact on the history of mankind.

